Follow the platform APIs that moved out from under it

Four calls the app makes into android.* no longer resolve. None of them is
LG's own: they are platform APIs that changed or went away between android 9
and 15, and every one of them throws an Error rather than the Exception the
surrounding try/catch is written for.

  IWindowManager.hasNavigationBar()      -> takes a display id now
  IWindowManager.getNavBarPosition()     -> gone entirely, no replacement
  DisplayManager.setTemporaryBrightness  -> now setBrightness(int, float)
  NotificationChannel.blockFields        -> the platform calls it lockFields
  WindowManager.LayoutParams.bypassKeyFlags -> LG's field, never existed here

hasNavigationBar and lockFields are the same call under a new shape, so they
are rewritten rather than dropped: the constants the channel is handed - 4,
0x10, 0x20, 0x80 - are USER_LOCKED_IMPORTANCE, VIBRATION, SOUND and
SHOW_BADGE, which is what lockFields takes.

getNavigationBarPosition returns the -1 its own catch clause returns, which
the caller reads as "not on either side" and lays out without side padding -
right for a bar along the bottom, and for gesture navigation.

setTemporaryBrightness took 0-255 for the default display; setBrightness
takes 0..1. The conversion is obvious but a wrong one blacks out the screen
mid-programme, so the call is dropped instead: the in-app brightness slider
does nothing and the screen stays where the system put it.

bypassKeyFlags is LG's own field on LayoutParams, ORed and masked in
eighteen places to let the test screens swallow the hardware keys. Nothing
reads it here, so the read-modify-writes go; the keys behave normally.

